The production version of the GT-R was unveiled at the [[Tokyo Motor Show#2007|2007 Tokyo Motor Show]], following an on-screen 7:38.54 minute lap time in the partially wet [[Nürburgring Nordschleife|Nürburgring ''Nordschleife'']] was broadcast, Nissan claimed it was the fastest lap time for a mass-produced car, beating the benchmark [[Porsche 997|Porsche 911 Turbo]]'s time of 7:40 minutes. The GT-R was previewed as "''A supercar, for Anyone, Anywhere, Anytime''".<ref name="NGV"/> In November, 2007, Nissan revealed that the car already had a considerable demand, as more than 3,000 buyers pre-ordered the car in Japan before its official launch. The car was expected to sell around 200 units per month, to maintain exclusivity.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Nissan GT-R is Officially Successful |url=https://www.motor1.com/news/2948/nissan-gt-r-is-officially-successful/ |access-date=2022-10-03 |website=Motor1.com |language=en}}</ref> The first production GT-R was purchased by Nissan's CEO at the time, [[Carlos Ghosn]].<ref name="NGV" /> Deliveries began on December 6, 2007, in Japan,<ref>{{cite press release |url=https://global.nissannews.com/ja-JP/releases/071024-01-j |title=「NISSAN GT-R」を発表|trans-title=NISSAN GT-R announced |website=Nissan|date=2007-10-24 |access-date=2026-06-07|language=ja}}</ref> on July 7, 2008, in North America and in March 2009 in Europe. The large disparity in initial marketing between these regional releases was due to Nissan having to build GT-R performance centres where the car was serviced.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Nissan Certified Collision Repair Network {{!}} Nissan USA |url=https://collision.nissanusa.com/gt-r-certified-repair/ |access-date=2023-01-18 |website=collision.nissanusa.com}}</ref>

==== 2009 update ====
In 2009, the GT-R received its first—of many—updates for the 2010 model year. It featured increased engine power output of {{cvt|485|PS|kW hp|0}} at 6,400 rpm, with no improvement in engine torque. The [[Launch control (automotive)|launch control]] system was reprogrammed to reduce stress in the transaxle and improve acceleration. It was also offered for sold 2009 models. Other upgrades include re-tuned suspension, updated wheel finish, standard front seat and side [[curtain airbag]]s.<ref>{{Cite web |title=2010 Nissan GT-R Review & Ratings |url=https://www.edmunds.com/nissan/gt-r/2010/review/ |access-date=2022-02-28 |website=Edmunds |language=en-us}}</ref>

=== 2010 facelift ===
{{multiple image
| align             = left
| direction         = vertical
| width             = 220
| image1            = Nissan GT-R (Facelift) – Frontansicht, 9. April 2011, Düsseldorf.jpg
| image2            = Nissan GT-R (Facelift) – Heckansicht, 9. April 2011, Düsseldorf.jpg
| caption2          = Facelifted 2010 (2011 model year) GT-R
| caption1          = 
}}

In 2010, for the 2011 model year, the GT-R received its first facelift. The car was revised and re-codenamed as "DBA–R35", replacing the original "CBA–R35". The revised version featured the same engine with altered mapping, changed valve timing, larger inlets and a modified exhaust system which boosts rated power to {{convert|390|kW|PS hp|0|abbr=on}} at 6,400 rpm and {{convert|607|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} of torque from 3,200 to 6,000 rpm. The chassis was stiffened by a more rigid front strut bar made out of carbon composite. Larger front brake rotors, lighter and stiffer wheels, and revised [[Dunlop Tyres|Dunlop]] tyres reduced unsprung weight by {{cvt|12|kg|lb|round=0.5}}. The suspensions contained stiffer springs, dampers and anti-roll bars and revised geometry to improve grip, new dampers provided more constant damping force.<ref>{{cite press release |url=https://usa.nissannews.com/en-US/releases/release-64e0518310384ed0968ca638a92d22cf-2012-nissan-gt-r-features-more-horsepower-new-black-edition-revised-fascias-and-carbon-fiber-interior-trim |title=Nissan and Infiniti – 2012 Nissan GT-R Features More Horsepower, New "Black Edition," Revised Fascias and Carbon Fiber Interior Trim |publisher=Nissan |location=US |date=November 17, 2010 |access-date=July 17, 2020}}</ref> Cosmetic changes reduced its drag coefficient to {{Cd|0.26|link=car}} and increased overall downforce by 10 percent. Achieved by a revised front bumper with integrated [[LED]]s, improving radiator cooling and front brakes cooling while reducing drag. The revised rear diffuser improved [[downforce]] and additional rear cooling ducts were placed for further brake cooling. The interior improved quality and comfort. Offered with a new HDD [[CarWings]] navigation system, with enhanced entertainment features and a USB port with iPod connectivity.<ref>{{Cite web|title=2011 Nissan GT-R Buyer's Guide: Reviews, Specs, Comparisons|url=https://www.motortrend.com/cars/nissan/gt-r/2011/|access-date=2021-08-31|website=MotorTrend|location=US}}</ref> Engine noise and road noise were reduced, and the overall performance of the car was improved. Overall weight of the car was decreased by {{cvt|5|kg|lb|0}}.<ref name="2012 Specs">{{cite web|url=https://usa.nissannews.com/en-US/releases/us-2012-nissan-gt-r-press-kit?selectedTabId=%23release-d2c523b919114c2db979970632ad9b97 |title=2012 Nissan GT-R Press Kit |date=April 22, 2012 |access-date=September 26, 2023}}</ref> The car went on sale in mid-November 2010 in Japan and February 2011 in Europe, North America, and in other regions.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.autoblog.com/2010/10/18/officially-official-ladies-and-gentlemen-your-2011-nissan-gt-r/|title=Officially Official: Ladies and Gentlemen, your 2011 Nissan GT-R|work=Autoblog |date=October 18, 2010|access-date=July 17, 2020}}</ref>

==== 2012 update ====
Similar to the update in 2009, the car was offered a revision in 2012, for the 2013 model year. It featured increased power output of {{cvt|550|PS|kW hp|0}} at 6,400 rpm and {{cvt|628|Nm|lbft|0}} of torque from 3,200 to 5,200 rpm. This was achieved by revised intake manifolds reducing air resistance, enlarged duct for the intercooler, new exhaust lowering back-pressure, and sodium-filled exhaust valves reducing the temperature of the combustion chambers. The ECU was remapped to alter valve timing, and improved air-fuel mixture ratio and ignition. A new flywheel housing, retuned suspension setup and revised weight distribution further increased performance and handling.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2016-03-23|title=2017 Nissan GT-R: The Refreshed R35.5 Debuts in New York|url=https://www.motortrend.com/news/2017-nissan-gt-r-refreshed-r35-5-debuts-new-york/|access-date=2021-08-30|website=MotorTrend|location=US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=2013 Nissan GT-R Buyer's Guide: Reviews, Specs, Comparisons|url=https://www.motortrend.com/cars/nissan/gt-r/2013/|access-date=2021-08-31|website=MotorTrend|location=US}}</ref>

==== 2014 update ====
{{multiple image
| align             = right
| direction         = vertical
| width             = 220
