| caption3          = Interior
}}

The GT-R received its second facelift in 2016, for the 2017 model year. The 2016 facelift GT-R was introduced at the [[New York International Auto Show#2016|2016 New York International Auto Show]].<ref>{{cite press release|title=Nissan Motor Co., Ltd. unveils the 2017 NISSAN GT-R at the New York International Auto Show|url=https://global.nissannews.com/en/releases/2017-nissan-gt-r-makes-world-debut-at-new-york-international-auto-show|date=2016-03-23|access-date=2026-06-07}}</ref> It was re-codenamed as "4BA–R35", featured the biggest changes in its 18-year production span. Redesigned front and rear facias improved engine and brake cooling, reshaped C-pillars reducing turbulence and strengthened hood reduced deformation at high speeds. Enhancements to engine cooling led to maintain turbo boost pressure dialed up, increasing engine power output to {{convert|570|PS|kW hp|0|abbr=on}} at 6,800&nbsp;rpm and {{convert|633|Nm || abbr=on}} of torque at 3,300–5,300&nbsp;rpm, maximum speed of the engine ([[redline]]) was increased from 7,000 to 7,100 rpm.<ref>{{Cite web|title=2017 Nissan GT-R Buyer's Guide: Reviews, Specs, Comparisons|url=https://www.motortrend.com/cars/nissan/gt-r/2017/|access-date=2021-08-31|website=MotorTrend|language=en}}</ref> Transmission was remapped to shift gears smoothly and quieter than before, and new titanium exhaust reduced weight. Revised chassis structure improved torsional rigidity by 5 percent. Noise insulation was provided by acoustic windscreen, sound deadening materials and Bose noise cancellation system. Furthermore, upgraded brakes, re-tuned dampers, lighter forged alloy wheels and stiffer chassis improved ride quality and cornering by less aggressive turn in. The interior featured a new leather material, steering wheel, 8-inch display infotainment system and a redesigned carbon-fibre centre console.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.caranddriver.com/reviews/a15100625/2017-nissan-gt-r-first-drive-review|title=2017 Nissan GT-R|last=Sherman|first=Don|date=2016-06-03|website=Car and Driver|language=en-US|access-date=2020-03-18}}</ref> Nissan claimed the upgraded GT-R increased acceleration at mid to high rpm ranges (3,200 rpm and above) and featured optimal balance between handling and ride comfort. The upgrades were also applied to Track Edition and Nismo variants, overall weight of the car was increased by {{cvt|40–55|kg|lb|0}} across all models.<ref name="2017 Specs">{{cite web|url=https://usa.nissannews.com/en-US/releases/us-2017-nissan-gt-r-press-kit?selectedTabId=%23release-7c539c9c95774f2d8162625ae21b3742 |title=2017 Nissan GT-R Press Kit |date=January 24, 2016 |access-date=September 26, 2023}}</ref>

==== 2019 update ====
[[File:Osaka Motor Show 2019 (162) - Nissan GT-R 50th Anniversary (4BA-R35).jpg|thumb|20-inch alloy wheels and the Bayside Blue body colour of the updated 2019 (2020 model year) GT-R]]

In 2019, for the 2020 model year, the car was offered with minor upgrades. Engine response and efficiency were improved by 5 percent due to the incorporation of revised turbocharges. The re-tuned transmission reduced its shifting time to 0.15 seconds, with gear selections happening during [[Anti-lock braking system|ABS]] engagement, it reduced understeer and improved cornering.<ref name=":4" /> Suspension and steering systems were also enhanced for improved handling. Cosmetic changes included new lightweight forged aluminium alloy wheels, interior and exterior colour options. Including the iconic Bayside Blue body colour, derived from the [[Nissan Skyline GT-R|Skyline GT-R R34]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=2020 Nissan GT-R Buyer's Guide: Reviews, Specs, Comparisons|url=https://www.motortrend.com/cars/nissan/gt-r/2020/|access-date=2021-08-31|website=MotorTrend|language=en}}</ref>

=== 2021–2022 hiatus ===
It was revealed the GT-R would not offer a 2022 model year in North America (mid-2021 to mid-2022 in calendar years), following the introduction of the T-spec variant. However, in Japan and other markets the car sold a 2022 model.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Yuan|first=Dave|date=2021-09-14|title=There will be no 2022 Nissan GT-R in America as history repeats with Millennium Jade|url=https://japanesenostalgiccar.com/nissan-gt-r-no-2022-model-year-in-us/|access-date=2022-02-22|website=Japanese Nostalgic Car|language=en-US}}</ref> [[Nissan Motor Australia|Nissan Australia]] discontinued the GT-R in Australia and New Zealand from October 31, 2021, as it was no longer eligible for new side impact regulations. The car was sold 1,021 units in Australia and 89 units in New Zealand, a total of 1,110 units were sold in the Australian market during 12-years of sales since its debut in April 2009.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-11-04 |title=Nissan GT-R sales surge as it reaches the end of the line in Australia |url=https://www.drive.com.au/news/nissan-gt-r-sales-surge-as-it-reaches-the-end-of-the-line-in-australia/ |access-date=2022-02-22 |website=Drive |language=en-AU}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Nissan GT-R R35 Australia + New Zealand VIN Table |url=https://gtr-registry.com/en-r35-au.php |access-date=2022-03-16 |website=gtr-registry.com |language=En}} {{dead link|date=June 2026}}</ref> Deliveries in Europe and United Kingdom were concluded in March 2022, for being unable to meet new noise regulations which took effect in June 2022.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Nissan GT-R Discontinued In Europe Because Of Emissions Regulations |url=https://www.motor1.com/news/573352/nissan-gt-r-discontinued-europe/ |access-date=2022-03-15 |website=Motor1.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-03-15 |title=Godzilla Is Dead In Europe As Nissan Is Axing GT-R Over Noise Regulations |url=https://www.carscoops.com/2022/03/nissan-gt-r-being-phased-out-in-europe-due-to-noise-regulations/ |access-date=2022-03-16 |website=Carscoops |language=en-US}}</ref> During its 13-years of sales since March 2009, Nissan sold more than 11,000 units in Europe.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Nissan GT-R R35 Europe + UK + Russia VIN Table |url=https://gtr-registry.com/en-r35-eu.php |access-date=2022-03-26 |website=gtr-registry.com |language=En |archive-date=March 17, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220317143755/https://gtr-registry.com/en-r35-eu.php |url-status=dead }}</ref>

In May 2022, Nissan officially closed orders in Japan, as it reached the planned sales amount.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Panait |first=Mircea |date=2022-05-04 |title=The JDM 2022 Nissan GT-R Is Sold Out |url=https://www.autoevolution.com/news/the-jdm-2022-nissan-gt-r-is-sold-out-188067.html |access-date=2022-09-27 |website=autoevolution |language=en}}</ref> It was revealed the car was no longer in sale in North America and Africa, as [[Nissan USA]], Nissan Canada, and [[Nissan South Africa]] official websites claimed the car was sold out.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Build & Price a Nissan GT-R |url=https://www.nissan.ca/shopping-tools/build-price?models=gt-r |access-date=2022-09-27 |website=Nissan Canada |language=en-CA}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=2021 Nissan GT-R Sports Car | work = Nissan USA |url=https://www.nissanusa.com/vehicles/sports-cars/gt-r.html |access-date=2022-09-27  |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Classen |first=Robin |date=2022-09-12 |title=Is this the end? Nissan's R35 GT-R is no longer sold as a brand-new car in SA |url=https://blog.gumtree.co.za/is-this-the-end-nissans-r35-gt-r-is-no-longer-sold-as-a-brand-new-car-in-sa/ |access-date=2022-10-03 |website=Gumtree Blog |language=en-US |archive-date=October 3, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221003071416/https://blog.gumtree.co.za/is-this-the-end-nissans-r35-gt-r-is-no-longer-sold-as-a-brand-new-car-in-sa/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

; Reintroduction
In October 2022, Nissan resumed production for the 2023 model year. Skipping the previous model year, the car was only offered in North America. No changes were reported compared to the previous model.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Rosales |first=Chris |date=2022-10-29 |title=The 2023 Nissan GT-R on Sale Again, Priced at $115,435, Somehow Beating Inflation |url=https://www.thedrive.com/news/the-2023-nissan-gt-r-on-sale-again-priced-at-115435-somehow-beating-inflation |access-date=2022-10-29 |website=The Drive |language=en}}</ref>

=== 2023 facelift ===
{{multiple image
