The car received its third and final facelift in 2023, for the 2024 model year, introduced at the [[Tokyo Auto Salon#2023|2023 Tokyo Auto Salon]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://global.nissannews.com/ja-JP/releases/230113-01-j |title=NISSAN GT‑R 2024年モデルを公開|trans-title=The 2024 model of the GT-R has been unveiled|work=nissan.co.jp |access-date=2023-01-13}}</ref> Nissan claimed the facelifted version would be limited to an unspecified number, and it was mainly offered only for the Japanese and North American markets, with three trim levels, Premium, Nismo and T-spec. The Track Edition variant was offered, exclusively in Japan.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www3.nissan.co.jp/vehicles/new/gt-r.html?rstid=20180330rst000000019&_gl=1*1u014mv*_gcl_au*MTE0NTE1ODY3MC4xNjk0NTk3NzA0 |title=2024 Nissan GT-R Sports Car |work=nissan.co.jp |access-date=2023-09-13}}</ref> Unlike its previous facelifts, this model featured minor cosmetic changes. Redesigned front and rear bumpers, front grille and rear wing, improved aerodynamics around the nose and rear diffuser, which increased downforce without increasing drag. No mechanical changes were made over the previous model year. In the North American market, the facelifted Premium and Nismo variants went on sale in the second and third quarter of 2023 respectively.<ref name=TH23>{{cite news |url=https://www.theautopian.com/the-2024-nissan-gt-r-surprises-everyone-with-a-facelift-declares-its-immortality/ |title=The 2024 Nissan GT-R Surprises Everyone With A Facelift, Declares Its Immortality |first=Thomas |last=Hundal |work=The Autopian |location=US |date=2023-01-12 |access-date=2023-01-14}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.autoblog.com/2023/01/12/2024-nissan-gt-r-reveal |title=2024 Nissan GT-R gets most dramatic upgrade in 7 years with new look and aero |first=Ben |last=Hsu |work=Autoblog |location=US |date=2023-01-12 |access-date=2023-01-14}}</ref> Sales in Japan began in March 20, 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The 2024 Nissan GT-R Is Now Available To Purchase In Japan From Nearly $105k |url=https://www.carscoops.com/2023/03/the-2024-nissan-gt-r-is-now-available-to-purchase-in-japan-from-nearly-105k/ |access-date=2023-03-23}}</ref>

=== Discontinuation ===
Nissan announced the end of sales for the North American market in October 2024 with two exclusive variants, the T-Spec Takumi and Skyline limited editions.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-06-14 |title=Nissan sunsets R35 generation GT-R with T-spec Takumi and Skyline limited editions |url=http://usa.nissannews.com/en-US/releases/nissan-sunsets-r35-generation-gt-r-with-t-spec-takumi-and-skyline-limited-editions |access-date=2024-06-15 |website=Official U.S. Newsroom |language=en-US}}</ref> Production continued mainly for the Japanese market until August 26, 2025, when the final unit—a Premium T-Spec edition finished in Midnight Purple—rolled off the production line in Tochigi.<ref name="Nissannews"/> Nissan officially announced the car's end of production, claimed the rising costs and stricter regulations invoked their decision, while also addressing the potential development of a next-generation GT-R.<ref name="Nissannews"/>

=== Production figures ===
During its 18 year production span, Nissan had produced around 48,000 units and sold in various different markets worldwide.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Nissan GT-R R35 Production Numbers|url=http://gtr-registry.com/en-r35-colours.php|access-date=2020-07-12|website=gtr-registry.com|language=En|archive-date=November 11, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171111080924/http://gtr-registry.com/en-r35-colours.php|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="Nissannews"/>

