=== Engine ===
[[File:Nissan VR38DETT Engine at Nissan Engine Museum (cropped).jpg|thumb|The ''VR38DETT'' engine]]
{{See also|Nissan VR engine}}

The GT-R was powered by the [[Nissan VR engine#VR38DETT|''VR38DETT'']] engine, a {{convert|3799|cc|L cuin|1|abbr=on}} 60° [[DOHC]] [[V6 engine|V6]] engine – which was shorter than traditional V6 production engines, benefiting weight distribution. The engine was based on the [[Nissan VQ engine|VQ engine]], but thoroughly modified to improve performance and suit the car.<ref name="orispecs"/> It featured an innovative hybrid [[Wet sump|wet]]/[[Dry sump|dry]]-sump pressurized lubrication system, controlled thermostatically to withstand high g-force and closed-deck construction increased stiffness. Cast iron cylinder liners were replaced by a 0.15&nbsp;mm (0.0059&nbsp;in) layer of plasma transferred wire arc sprayed cylinder bores, reducing friction for the piston rings to slide on smoothly.<ref name=":4">{{cite web |url=https://www.nissanusa.com/vehicles/sports-cars/gt-r/specs/ |title=2020 Nissan GT-R Specs |website=www.nissanusa.com |access-date=22 May 2020}}</ref> Two parallel [[IHI Corporation|Ishikawajima-Harima]] (IHI) ''RHF55'' turbochargers provided forced induction, boosting more than 0.7{{nbsp}}bar of boost and its turbine housing was integrated into the exhaust manifolds to minimize lag, decrease weight and improve vehicle balance.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.autoblog.com/2008/02/05/top-turbo-cars/ |title=Top 10 Turbo Cars |work=Autoblog |date=5 February 2008 |access-date=22 May 2020}}</ref> The engine was originally rated at {{convert|480|PS|kW hp|0|abbr=on|order=out}} at 6,400{{nbsp}}rpm and {{convert|430|lbft|Nm lbft|0|abbr=on|order=out}} of torque at 3,200{{ndash}}5,200{{nbsp}}rpm, which made it the most powerful engine fitted to a Japanese production vehicle at the time.<ref>{{Cite web|title=2009 Nissan GT-R|url=https://www.autoguide.com/manufacturer/nissan/2009-nissan-gt-r-819.html |access-date=2023-09-28 |website=autoguide.com|date=October 21, 2008 }}</ref> The engine was compliant with [[California Air Resources Board]] [[Ultra Low Emission Vehicle]] (ULEV) standards.<ref name="orispecs"/> It featured 24 valves controlled by dual overhead camshafts (2 per cylinder head) with intake only variable valve timing. It also featured a feedback control system which changes air fuel ratio depending on the engine load which reduces fuel consumption. Fully equipped with catalytic converters, turbochargers, engine accessories, front [[Differential (mechanical device)|differential]] assembly and turbo outlet pipes, the engine weighed {{convert|276|kg|lb|0|abbr=on}}.<ref>{{Cite web|title=2009gtr.com: VR38DETT engine weight|url=https://www.2009gtr.com/2008/02/vr38dett-engine-weight.html|access-date=2022-02-20|website=2009gtr.com}}</ref> Notable features of the ''VR38DETT'' include:

* Continuously variable valve timing control system ([[CVTCS]]) on intake valve.
* Aluminium [[cylinder block]] with high-endurance/low-friction plasma-sprayed bores.
* Iridium-tipped [[spark plug]]s.
* Electronic drive-by-wire throttle.
* Multi port fuel injection.
* Pressurized lubrication system with thermostatically controlled cooling and magnesium oil sump.
* Fully symmetrical dual intake and low back-pressure exhaust system.
* Secondary air intake system to rapidly heat catalysts to peak cleaning efficiency.
* 50 State LEV2/ULEV.

{{Listen
| type         = sound
| filename     = Nissan_GT-R_SpecV.ogg
| title        = ''VR38DETT'' engine note
}}

The engines were hand built in a dust proof, temperature controlled room at Nissan's [[Yokohama]] plant, by only nine "''Takumi Craftsmen''" mechanics throughout the car's 18 year production span. Names of these mechanics were badged on every engine built. Each engine took approximately 6 hours and 300 components to be fully assembled.<ref name="Nissannews"/> Approximately 13 engines were built per day in a single shift. The cars were built at [[Tochigi Prefecture|Tochigi]] plant on a shared production line. At the end of the production process, the cars were test driven by professional drivers around Nissan's test track.<ref>{{cite web |title=Takumi: The Master Craftsmen behind Each Nissan GT-R |url=https://reports.nissan-global.com/EN/?p=10948 |publisher=Nissan |access-date=19 May 2020 |date=25 July 2013 |archive-date=October 19, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171019064038/http://reports.nissan-global.com/EN/?p=10948 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite press release |url=https://usa.nissannews.com/en-US/releases/release-c34e9c7b2b6d4ec18aba0c695d521b4f |title=Nissan's Legendary Takumi: Four Master Craftsmen Who Hand-Build Every Nissan GT-R Engine |publisher=Nissan |location=US |date=16 September 2013 |access-date=19 May 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite episode |title=Nissan |series=[[Ultimate Factories]] |season=6 |number=12 |date=15 November 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Dalle Carbonare |first1=Dino |title=Car Builder>> How The Nissan Gt-r Is Built |url=http://www.speedhunters.com/2009/07/car_builder_gt_gt_how_the_nissan_gt_r_is_built/ |website=Speedhunters |access-date=19 May 2020 |date=28 July 2009 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20180812042324/http://www.speedhunters.com/2009/07/car_builder_gt_gt_how_the_nissan_gt_r_is_built/ |archive-date=12 August 2018 |url-status= live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Perry |first1=Joanne |title=Nissan Tochigi's human touch |url=https://automotivemanufacturingsolutions.com/focus/the-human-touch |website=Automotive Manufacturing Solutions |date=14 August 2017 |access-date=19 May 2020}}</ref>

