=== Drivetrain ===
[[File:Nissan GT-R Premium Midship Package.jpg|thumb|''ATTESA E-TS Pro'' all-wheel-drive system]]
{{See also|ATTESA E-TS}}

The GT-R was the first ever rear mounted independent transaxle all-wheel-drive vehicle. The transaxle consist of the transmission, clutches, centre and rear differential. Placed on the rear axle, it helped balance the weight of the engine up front and improved the front-to-rear weight distribution to 54:46.<ref>{{Cite press release |date=2007-10-24|title=Nissan Announces New Nissan GT-R|url=https://global.nissannews.com/en/releases/071024-01-e|access-date=2021-09-17 |publisher=Nissan |location=US}}</ref> The car featured an exclusively developed, six-speed [[BorgWarner]] co-designed ''GR6Z30A'' type [[dual-clutch transmission]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Brooke |first=Lindsay |date=5 February 2008 |title=BorgWarner DCT for Nissan's GT-R |url=http://www.sae.org/automag/technewsletter/080205Tech/04.htm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081031232522/http://www.sae.org/automag/technewsletter/080205Tech/04.htm |archive-date=31 October 2008 |access-date=22 May 2020 |website=[[SAE International|www.sae.org]]}}</ref> It was hand built by Takumi Craftsmen in a specialized room, similar to the engine assembly room, at Nissan's Aichi Kikai plant in [[Nagoya]]. With a shift time of 0.15–0.2 seconds, it was [[Shift time#Example upshift times|one of the fastest gear shifting transmissions]] fitted to a production vehicle. Nissan claimed their choice of a DCT over the traditional [[manual transmission]] was because of its significant performance advantage, high demand, better fuel efficiency, favouring the advanced technologies featured in the car, and being the precise choice for meeting regulation and emission standards. It was used in conjunction with the unique ''ATTESA E-TS Pro'' all-wheel-drive system, providing power to all four wheels with minimal loss and controlled by the built-in [[Vehicle Dynamics Control]] (VDC) to aid in handling and stability.<ref name="Oagana">{{cite web|last=Oagana|first=Alex|date=5 May 2011|title=Nissan GT-R's Secret - ATTESA E-TS AWD System Explained|url=https://www.autoevolution.com/news/nissan-gt-r-s-secret-attesa-e-ts-awd-system-explained-35006.html|access-date=22 May 2020|website=www.autoevolution.com}}</ref><ref name="orispecs2">{{cite web|title=2009 Nissan GT-R Specs and Standard Features|url=http://www.gtrnissan.com/specs.en.us.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080914214129/http://www.gtrnissan.com/specs.en.us.html|archive-date=14 September 2008|access-date=22 May 2020|website=www.gtrnissan.com}}</ref> Three shift modes were available in the drive mode selector: "R-mode" provided maximum performance of the six-speed dual-clutch transmission, "Normal-mode" was recommended for daily driving, and "Save-mode" was recommended for maximizing fuel economy, and driving on slippery surfaces, such as on snow or ice. Gear shifting was controlled automatically or rather manually through paddle shifters.<ref name="orispecs" />

The ''ATTESA ET-S Pro'' all-wheel-drive system used in the GT-R, was a different implementation to the version used in Skyline GT-R models. Unlike the previous versions which relied heavily on mechanical feedback, this updated system used hydraulically actuated clutches and electronic sensors. It was a rear biased all-wheel-drive system, providing incredible handling and stability at high speeds. In normal conditions, it sent 100 percent power to the rear wheels through the main [[Carbon-fiber-reinforced polymers|carbon-composite]] propeller shaft. In situations with limited traction available to the rear wheels, such as in heavy acceleration and cornering, the separated additional propeller shaft sent torque from the transfer case to the front differential, resulting in a 50:50 front to rear power distribution. An open type differential distributed power in the front, and a 1.5 way multi-disc mechanical [[limited-slip differential]] did so in the rear. In addition to these mechanical components, the car was also capable of managing slip angles. Automatically braking either of the front wheels occurs when slip is detected in order to send power to the other front wheel, so as to mimic the functionality of a mechanical limited slip differential.<ref name="Oagana"/> The Vehicle Dynamic Control (VDC), was offered with three different modes: "R-mode" for performance driving conditions such as on track and dragstrip, lowering traction control sensitivity but did not fully turn off, "Normal-mode" for daily driver use, and "Off-mode" which turned off both traction control and electronic stability control entirely.

