The GT-R had a [[kerb weight]] of {{convert|3837|–|3935|lb|kg lb|0|abbr=on|order=out}}<ref name="swan">{{cite web |url=https://www.caranddriver.com/reviews/a17012264/2009-nissan-gt-r-first-drive-review/ |title=2009 Nissan GT-R |last=Swan |first=Tony |date=1 December 2007 |website=www.caranddriver.com |access-date=23 May 2020}}</ref><ref name="loh">{{cite web |url=https://www.motortrend.com/cars/nissan/gt-r/2008/2008-nissan-gt-r-2/ |title=First Look: 2008 Nissan GTR |last=Loh |first=Edward |date=25 October 2007 |website=www.motortrend.com |access-date=23 May 2020}}</ref> distributed 54 percent to the front and 46 percent to the rear, providing greater stability. It was the first and so far the only model built on the Premium Midship (PM) platform, an evolution of the [[Nissan FM platform|Front Midship (FM)]] architecture introduced in the [[Nissan Skyline#V35|Skyline (V35)]]/[[Infiniti G35]]. Its chassis utilized a hybrid unibody construction using a combination of steel, carbon fibre, and die-cast aluminium. It primarily used a conventional steel monocoque but with [[Alcoa]] aluminium subframes and suspensions. The door frames were die-cast aluminium, and the bumpers were made of lightweight polypropylene. The front cross-member, radiator support and rear diffuser were made of carbon-fibre to strengthen the structure and reduce weight. The majority of body panels were made of aluminium sheets, such as the bonnet, boot lid and door shells. Outer body panels were stamped using a multiple-strike coining process in order to add rigidity and precision.<ref>{{cite press release |url=http://nissannews.com/en-CA/nissan/canada/presskits/ca-2014-nissan-gt-r-press-kit |title=2014 Nissan GT-R Press Kit |publisher=Nissan |location=Canada |quote=The Nissan GT-R's unique Premium Midship platform, created expressly for use with the GT-R... |access-date=October 21, 2017 }}</ref>

The car was fitted with an [[active suspension#Solenoid/valve actuated|active suspension]] system, called "DampTronic", which contained [[Bilstein Shock Absorbers|Bilstein]] designed electronic adaptive dampers and manufactured by Yorozu Corporation. Onboard computers adjust the suspension components every one hundredth of a second, evenly distributing the mass for all four wheels and maximizing tyre contact for better grip, under heavy acceleration, braking and cornering. The GT-R was the first production vehicle to feature such a suspension system.<ref>{{Citation|title=National Geographic Nissan GTR Full Documentary 720p| date=April 6, 2017 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=woHRGJ-nXRw|language=en|access-date=2022-02-20}}</ref> The DampTronic system allowed the driver to select three different suspension modes from the mode selector: "R-mode" for maximum performance required on track and dragstrip, "Normal-mode" for daily driver use, and "Comfort-mode", which softened the dampers to improve ride quality. The car featured 20-inch 15-spoke forged lightweight aluminium alloy wheels manufactured by [[Rays Engineering|Rays]], wrapped up with exclusively developed [[Dunlop Tyres|Dunlop]] SP Sport Maxx GT 600 DSST CTT [[run-flat tyres]], [[Bridgestone Potenza]] RE070R run-flat tyres were offered in earlier models. Each wheel featured a [[knurling]] around the inside of the rim in order to keep the tyres from slipping on the rim due to heavy acceleration and braking.<ref>{{Cite press release |title=2019 Nissan GT-R Press Kit |url=https://usa.nissannews.com/en-US/releases/us-2019-nissan-gt-r-press-kit |publisher=Nissan |location=US |date=2018-12-05 |access-date=2022-02-20}}</ref> The brake setup featured [[Brembo]] monoblock six-piston [[Disc brake|ventilated brake callipers]] with {{cvt|15.35|inch|mm|0|order=flip}} at the front, and four-piston callipers with {{cvt|15.0|inch|mm|0|order=flip}} full floating, cross drilled two piece rotors in the rear. They accompanied with low steel and high stiffness brake pads. [[Carbon ceramic]] rotors were offered in some of the special variants, which were the largest brake rotors ever fitted to a Japanese production vehicle.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2008-09-14 |title=2009 Nissan GT-R Specs and Standard Features |url=http://www.gtrnissan.com/specs.en.us.html |access-date=2022-02-20 |website= |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080914214129/http://www.gtrnissan.com/specs.en.us.html |archive-date=14 September 2008 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=GT: GT {{!}} R braking systems|url=https://www.brembo.com/en/car/sporting-use/gt-r-braking-system|access-date=2022-02-20|website=Brembo – Official WebSite|language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Exterior ===
[[File:Nissan GTR - Flickr - Alexandre Prévot (12).jpg|thumb|Rear view of the GT-R, showing signature four round taillights, first offered in 1972 on the Skyline C110|alt=]]

According to chief design director, [[Shiro Nakamura]], "aero blade" front fenders, blackened A-pillars and the slopping roofline of the GT-R were inspired by the [[Real Robot|giant robots]] of the ''[[Gundam]]'' series.<ref name="businessweek2007">{{cite news|url=http://www.businessweek.com/innovate/content/nov2007/id20071119_902449.htm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071129203305/http://www.businessweek.com/innovate/content/nov2007/id20071119_902449.htm |url-status=dead |archive-date=November 29, 2007 |title=Nissan Redesigns a Japanese Icon |work=BusinessWeek |first=Matt |last=Vella |date=November 26, 2007<!-- , 3:57&nbsp;pm EST -->|access-date=October 27, 2008}}</ref> Nakamura stated: "The GT-R is unique because it is not simply a copy of a European-designed sports car; it had to really reflect [[Japanese culture]]". Nissan's American designers sculpted the rear three-quarters of the vehicle, while their European designers sculpted the roofline.<ref name="businessweek2007"/> Nissan developed a 6-stage paint process with a double clear coat and chip-resistant paint for use in critical areas of the chassis. An optional liquid-effect finish employed a hand-polished 8-stage process with product-specific colours, such as the original Super Silver metallic paint, with three layers of [[Automotive paint#Clearcoat|clearcoat]].

The car featured an overall muscular, sharp and a boxy body design. Despite being a bulkier body design compared to its rivals, it was high-[[downforce]] and less–[[Drag (physics)|drag]] through its smoother air flow over the overall body. Up front, [[NACA duct]]s on the hood scooped in clean air in order cool the engine compartment. Side front fender air vents also improved the airflow around the tyres, while cooling the engine. Front bumper edges were sharpened and featured air ducts and vents to improve downforce while cooling the brakes.<ref name="orispecs" /> Overall front end of the car featured an aggressive look, the rear end carries on the signature four round taillights from its predecessors. The rear end also featured air ducts and vents for further brake cooling and generated a smoother air flow to minimize drag. A body-coloured [[Spoiler (car)|rear spoiler]], whereas some special editions featured a dry carbon-fibre rear spoiler, in order improve rear downforce. The car featured a 315-litre rear boot, an [[Underframe|under body]] made out of carbon-fibre panels, smoothens the air flow under the car.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Nissan GT-R - Car Body Design|url=https://www.carbodydesign.com/archive/2007/11/09-nissan-gt-r/|access-date=2022-02-21|language=en-US}}</ref> The car was fitted with [[Light-emitting diode|LED]] headlights, automatic on/off headlights, LED daytime running lights, LED taillights and brake lights, dual-heated body-colour power wing mirrors, power-folding side mirrors, flush-mounted aluminium door handles, four 5-inch titanium exhaust outlets with polished tips and UV-reducing solar glass.<ref name="nissanusa1">[http://www.nissanusa.com/gt-r/specifications.html 2013 Nissan GT-R | Specifications]. Nissan USA.</ref> The GT-R generated more than {{cvt|80|kg|lb|0}} of downforce at {{cvt|300|km/h|mph|0}} on both ends, while maintaining a {{Cd|long=yes|link=car|0.26–0.27}}. Becoming the first production car to feature such an aerodynamic efficiency.<ref>{{cite press release |url=https://france.nissannews.com/fr-FR/releases/release-1575-nissan-gt-r-la-supercar-en-toute-circonstance |title=NISSAN GT-R : LA SUPERCAR EN TOUTE CIRCONSTANCE |language=fr |publisher=Nissan |location=France |date=24 October 2007 |access-date=22 May 2020 |archive-date=July 28, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190728040526/https://france.nissannews.com/fr-FR/releases/release-1575-nissan-gt-r-la-supercar-en-toute-circonstance |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.caranddriver.com/reviews/a15148166/2009-nissan-gt-r-first-drive-review-1/ |title=2009 Nissan GT-R: A Gripping Blend of Speed and Technology |date=February 2008 |language=en |publisher=[[Car and Driver]] |access-date=2023-11-10}}</ref>

=== Interior ===
[[File:Nissan GT-R 06.JPG|thumb|Interior (pre-facelift)]]

The GT-R featured a hand stitched premium [[leather]] interior, embedded on the dashboard, steering wheel, door panels, centre console and seats. ''[[Motor Trend]]'' claimed the car featured one of the most finely crafted interiors despite its marginally lower price.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2018-06-07|title=Six Aesthetically Pleasing Interior Details on the 2018 Nissan GT-R Premium|url=https://www.motortrend.com/features/2018-nissan-gt-r-premium-interior/|access-date=2022-02-20|website=MotorTrend|language=en}}</ref> Carbon-fibre was used in the centre console and in the gauge cluster, which featured an analog speedometer, fuel meter and tachometer with a redline of 7,000–7,100 rpm. Digital displays featured traveling speed, gear, fuel economy and distance calculations. GT-R badged steering wheel contains volume controls and cruise control buttons. Magnesium [[paddle shifters]] were on the wheel column in earlier models and on the steering wheel in post 2017 facelifted models. The instrument cluster was angled towards the driver, featuring [[air conditioning]] controls, audio set-up controls and drive mode selectors. Centre console featured the engine start/off button, leather–wrapped shift lever, parking brake and an armrest with a small storage area inside and a [[USB]] port. The car was fitted with an 11-speaker [[Bose Corporation|Bose]] sound system.

[[File:2009 Nissan GT-R Black Edition - Flickr - The Car Spy (18).jpg|thumb|The customizable multifunction display integrated into the dash, had its roots in the Skyline GT-R R34]]
[[Polyphony Digital]], creators of the [[Gran Turismo (series)|''Gran Turismo'']] motor racing video games, were contracted to design the [[Multi-function display|multifunction display]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.edmunds.com/insideline/do/Features/articleId=123066 |title=Edmunds Inside Line: A Day in Germany With the GT-R Engineers |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071018104650/http://www.edmunds.com/insideline/do/Features/articleId=123066 |archive-date=October 18, 2007}}</ref> It featured a detailed log of the driving behavior, speed, g-force, fuel economy and more. Details of mechanical information such as the turbo boost, water and engine oil pressure, transmission oil, front and rear drive distribution, steering angles, braking and acceleration pressure, and optimal gearshift mapping for better fuel economy, satellite navigation and audio controls as well.<ref>{{Cite web|title=NISSAN GT-R press information...|url=https://www.nissan-global.com/NISSANGTR/0710/ENGLISH/DESIGN/index3.html|access-date=2022-02-20|website=www.nissan-global.com|archive-date=November 25, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201125222352/https://www.nissan-global.com/NISSANGTR/0710/ENGLISH/DESIGN/index3.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>
{{clear}}

== Variants ==
Alongside the standard model, four distinct trim levels were introduced over time, each bringing notable performance improvements and cosmetic updates. The lineup began with the Black Edition in 2012, followed by the track-focused Track Edition in 2013, Nismo in 2014, and T-spec trim in 2021.

