| A higher-powered and track focused GT-R Nismo was announced in February 2013 to be available for consumers in the near future.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://global.nissannews.com/en/releases/release-a814ecc066cfad869261b8340886df9f-130226-02-e |title=Nissan confirms new high performance GT-R Nismo coming soon |work=Nissan Newsroom |location=Japan |date=2013-02-25 |access-date=2023-09-04}}</ref>  Then CEO, Carlos Ghosn insisted that the car should set the lap record for mass-produced cars at the [[Nürburgring Nordschleife]] before its official launch. In May, development begun with Nismo engineers and drivers from the GT3 racing program. Numerous testing sessions took place in the [[Sendai Hi-Land Raceway]] and Nürburgring. At the end of September, development was finalized and the car set a lap time of 7:08.679 minutes.<ref name="GNDV" /> The GT-R Nismo officially made its public debut at the 2013 Tokyo Motor Show. It offered improved handling and acceleration, providing {{cvt|100|kg|lb|0}} more [[downforce]] at {{cvt|300|km/h|mph|0}} compared to the standard car, and chassis rigidity was improved by 8 percent. Kerb weight was reduced to 1,730 kg (3,814 lb).<ref name="2015 Specs" /><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.carsales.com.au/editorial/details/tokyo-motor-show-nissan-gt-r-nismo-40084/ |title=TOKYO MOTOR SHOW: Nissan GT-R Nismo |work=carsales |location=Australia |date=2013-11-20 |access-date=2020-07-04}}</ref>

2019 update further improved the performance of the car. More downforce, acceleration from 0 to 80 km/h (0 to 50 mph) was quicker by 2.4 m (8 ft), and the Nürburgring lap time was claimed to be 5 seconds quicker compared to the previous year, due to a 5 percent improvement in cornering force and braking performance.<ref name="MotorTrend-2020">{{cite news |url=https://www.motortrend.com/reviews/2020-nissan-gt-r-nismo-first-drive-review/ |title=2020 Nissan GT-R Nismo First Drive: The Art of Continuous Improvement |last=MacKenzie |first=Angus |website=Motor Trend |location=US |date=July 8, 2019 |access-date=August 8, 2019}}</ref> Kerb weight was rated at 1,750 kg (3,858 lb).<ref name="2020 Specs">{{cite web|url=https://usa.nissannews.com/en-US/releases/2020-nissan-gt-r-press-kit?selectedTabId=%23release-ec70db82a191344651ecdb57e205df9e |title=2020 Nissan GT-R Press Kit |date=May 14, 2019 |access-date=September 26, 2023}}</ref> This upgraded version was 2.5 seconds quicker than the previous year around their test track.<ref>{{Cite web|title=2020 Nissan GT-R Nismo deep dive: How it's the fastest Godzilla ever|url=https://www.motorauthority.com/news/1124854_2020-nissan-gt-r-nismo-deep-dive-how-it-s-the-fastest-godzilla-ever|access-date=2021-09-10|website=Motor Authority|date=March 2020 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.nissanusa.com/vehicles/future-concept/2020-gt-r.html |title=2020 Nissan GT-R Sports Car |publisher=Nissan |location=US |access-date=April 20, 2019}}</ref>

2023 update saw major performance upgrades. Cornering was improved with increased downforce by 13 percent over the previous year.<ref name="GT-R Story">{{Cite web |title=GT-R Story |url=https://www3.nissan.co.jp/vehicles/new/gt-r/performance_safety/history.html |access-date=2023-09-14 |website=nissan.co.jp |language=ja}}</ref> The Nismo Appearance Package offered special exterior features of the GT-R Nismo Special Edition. This included the exclusive Stealth Grey body colour, clear coated carbon-fibre hood, exclusive engine cover and the red-accented Nismo wheels.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-01-13 |title=2024 Nissan GT-R Press Kit |url=https://usa.nissannews.com/en-US/releases/2024-nissan-gt-r-press-kit |access-date=2023-01-18 |website=Official U.S. Newsroom |language=en-US}}</ref>
