== Performance ==
[[File:FoS20162016 0625 104335AA (27290312054).jpg|thumb|The GT-R at Goodwood Festival of Speed]]

The GT-R was considered heavy and bulky compared to its competitors, its innovative [[all-wheel-drive]] and [[active suspension]] systems utilised its weight as an advantage. The unconventional body design maximized [[downforce]] and minimized [[Drag (physics)|drag]], resulting in exceptional acceleration, cornering and handling capabilities. Many automotive publications have used the term, "the GT-R defies the laws of physics" to describe its performance.<ref>[https://www.motorauthority.com/news/1032412_nissan-gt-r-sets-first-recorded-time-for-the-worlds-greatest-driving-road ''Motor Authority''] [https://autocape.co.za/nissan-gt-r-godzilla-remains-sa-favourite/ ''Autocape''] [https://www.caranddriver.com/nissan/gt-r ''Car and Driver''] [https://www.drive.com.au/reviews/2012-nissan-gt-r-review/ ''The Drive''] [https://www.motortrend.com/news/2021-nissan-gt-r-pricing-track-edition-discontinued/ ''Motor Trend'']</ref>

Originally, Nissan claimed the GT-R can attain a top speed of {{convert|196|mi/h|km/h|abbr=on|order=flip}},<ref name=":11">{{cite web|url=http://www.edmunds.com/insideline/do/News/articleId=123047#3 |title=Breaking News: Undisguised 2009 Nissan GT-R |access-date=January 16, 2008 |work=edmunds.com |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071024133643/http://www.edmunds.com/insideline/do/News/articleId=123047 |archive-date=October 24, 2007}}</ref> but ''[[Motor Trend]]'' recorded a top speed of {{convert|195.0|mi/h|km/h|abbr=on|order=flip}} with the original 2009 model year GT-R.<ref name="MTCompare">{{cite web |url=https://www.motortrend.com/cars/nissan/gt-r/2009/2009-corvette-zr1-vs-porsche-gt2-vs-ferrari-599-vs-nissan-gtr/ |title=War of the Worlds: ZR1 vs GT2 vs 599 vs GT-R |access-date=January 11, 2019 |last=St. Antoine |first=Arthur |date=October 27, 2008 |work=[[Motor Trend]] |publisher=Source Interlink Media}}</ref> [[Edmunds (company)|Edmunds]] held the first ever performance test using a customer-spec GT-R in Japan, achieving a 0-{{convert|60|mph|km/h|abbr=on|order=flip}} time of 3.3 seconds and a quarter-mile time of 11.6 seconds. It was the quickest production car they had ever tested.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yDoJdQXmwu8 |title=Nissan GT-R: World's 1st Full Test |website=[[YouTube]] |date=December 21, 2007 |access-date=October 14, 2023}}</ref> The car was capable of achieving superior performance figures in tests conducted by other automotive magazines.<ref name="MTCompare" /><ref name="C&D09">{{Cite web|last=Robinson|first=Aaron|date=2021-03-30|title=Tested: 2009 Nissan GT-R Defies Expectations|url=https://www.caranddriver.com/reviews/a15151521/2009-nissan-gt-r-road-test|access-date=2022-02-08|website=Car and Driver|language=en-US}}</ref> Owners expressed concerns that duplicating the times achieved in these tests would void their factory warranty. Chief Engineer, Kazutoshi Mizuno indicated that he has never used the term "launch control", which refers to the act of turning off [[Vehicle Dynamic Control|Vehicle Dynamic Control (VDC)]] and launching the car at around 4,500 rpm.<ref name="mizuno">{{cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=w1bHGaV-Lvw  |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211215/w1bHGaV-Lvw |archive-date=2021-12-15 |url-status=live|title=2010 GT-R Launch Control Controversy Explained on video |publisher=GT Channel |access-date=April 19, 2019 |via=YouTube}}{{cbignore}}</ref> However, then-Nissan director of product planning officer, John Wiener stated in an interview with [[Jay Leno]] that "we [Nissan] actually offer a 'launch mode'".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5HwRZhPVmQ4  |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131207075810/http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5HwRZhPVmQ4 |archive-date=2013-12-07 |url-status=dead|title=Jay Leno's Garage: GT-R Review |access-date=August 11, 2008 |publisher=YouTube}}</ref> The GT-R user's manual states that turning off the VDC is only meant for escaping low-traction situations such as mud or snow. However the manufacturer themselves used to turn off the VDC during official hot laps and time attacks. In 2010, Nissan re-programmed the GT-R to reduce engine speed at launch to around 3,500–4,000 rpm with VDC enabled, which was meant to improve acceleration times. The revised programming was also installed in unsold 2009 model year models that were still in their inventory, was also available for existing 2009 vehicles.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.automotiveaddicts.com/2126/nissan-gt-r-launch-control-reprogram-clarification-from-nissan|title=Nissan GT-R Launch Control Reprogram Clarification from Nissan|date=January 30, 2009|first=Malcolm|last=Hogan|website=Automotiveaddicts.com|access-date=April 28, 2019}}</ref>

The re-programmed GT-R had a launch mode called "R-Mode Start". Enabled by pushing the three buttons up, transmission, suspension and VDC. The system allowed a maximum of 4 consecutive hard launches before locking itself out, after which can be unlocked by driving normally for {{cvt|1.5|mile|km|1|abbr=on|order=flip}}. Combined with the "R-Mode Start", the GT-R retained its original performance, which set the [[Guinness World Records|Guinness World Record]] for the fastest accelerating four-seater production vehicle.<ref name=":8">{{Cite web |date=2009-06-30 |title=2009 Nissan GT-R Declared Fastest Accelerating Production Four-Seater By Guinness |url=https://www.drive.com.au/news/2009-nissan-gt-r-declared-fastest-accelerating-production-four-seater-by-guinness/ |access-date=2022-03-21 |website=Drive |language=en-AU}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2008-03-31 |title=Godzilla Unleashed! 2009 Nissan GTR acceleration test |url=https://www.motortrend.com/reviews/2009-nissan-gtr-acceleration-test/ |access-date=2023-10-09 |website=[[Motor Trend]] |language=en}}</ref> Nissan offered numerous upgrades for later model years, which saw significant improvements in acceleration figures, top speed, and Nürburgring lap times.<ref name="MotorTrend2011">{{cite web|url=http://www.motortrend.com/features/auto_news/2011/1111_2013_nissan_gt_r_10_second_car/|title=2013 Nissan GT-R is a 10 Second Car!|date=November 7, 2011 }}</ref>

